[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Public/Private Key Cryptography & Signatures in Web3"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Asymmetric cryptography is the foundational security layer of Web3. Every Ethereum address, Solana account, and digital transaction relies on public and private key pairs to prove ownership and authorize state changes without revealing sensitive data."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Concepts: Public vs. Private Keys"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A private key is a randomly generated 256-bit number that acts as your master password. Anyone with access to your private key controls your funds and smart contract interactions. A public key is mathematically derived from the private key using elliptic curve multiplication. In Web3, your wallet address is typically a shortened, hashed version of your public key."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Private Key: Kept secret, used to sign transactions and messages."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Public Key: Shared openly, used to verify signatures and derive addresses."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"ECDSA (secp256k1) in EVM Ecosystems"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Ethereum and Bitcoin utilize the Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (ECDSA) specifically over the secp256k1 curve. ECDSA produces signatures composed of two values, often denoted as r and s, alongside a recovery identifier v. This recovery ID allows smart contracts to easily recover the public key directly from the signature."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"const { ethers } = require("ethers");

// Generate a random wallet (private and public key pair)
const wallet = ethers.Wallet.createRandom();
console.log("Address:", wallet.address);
console.log("Private Key:", wallet.privateKey);

// Signing a message
const message = "Hello Web3";
wallet.signMessage(message).then((signature) => {
    console.log("Signature:", signature);
});"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Ed25519 in High-Performance Chains (Solana, Aptos)"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern high-throughput blockchains like Solana and Aptos heavily favor Ed25519, specifically over the Curve25519. Ed25519 offers high security margins, resistance to side-channel attacks, and significantly faster signature verification performance compared to secp256k1, making it ideal for high-TPS environments."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"const { Keypair } = require("@solana/web3.js");
const nacl = require("tweetnacl");

// Generate Solana Keypair
const keypair = Keypair.generate();
console.log("Public Key:", keypair.publicKey.toBase58());

// Signing a payload
const message = new TextEncoder().encode("Hello Solana");
const signature = nacl.sign.detached(message, keypair.secretKey);
console.log("Signature length:", signature.length);"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Security Best Practices for Web3 Developers"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Never hardcode private keys in repository source code or frontend client bundles."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always use cryptographically secure pseudo-random number generators (CSPRNG) when initializing keypairs."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Implement typed structured data signing (such as EIP-712 in Ethereum) to protect users from signing malicious raw payloads."}]
